% holding-decision-note: Held as a link only (README, "Cultures that protect their music"): the guide's terms cover learning and performing, not copying the score here.
% holding-blocked: Still needed: the composer's written permission before any notation, words or audio are posted on the site (the guide allows learning, performing and sharing for education, not reposting); and a review by the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre or by the composer of the credit, the story and how the class sings it. Neither has happened. Until then the class learns it from the CBC and MusiCounts pages.
% holding-kind: link
% holding-kind-note: No notes or words by design: the composer, CBC Music and MusiCounts share the song for teaching on their own pages, under terms that allow learning, performing and sharing for education but not reposting. Link to their pages.
% source-url: https://www.cbc.ca/music/events/canadian-music-class-challenge/the-story-behind-human-the-official-anthem-for-the-year-of-indigenous-languages-1.5299057 CBC Music: the story behind "Human", with teaching videos (not read here; CBC refuses automated readers)
% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/MusiCounts_Singing_In_Indigenous_Languages_final.pdf MusiCounts and CBC Music, Singing in Indigenous Languages (2024), pp. 4 and 6: the song and the terms
% performance-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gMLLz8MIKGI Human by Twin Flames (Twin Flames Music, YouTube)
% dossier-function: The 2019 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song. The guide says it "was written as the official anthem for UNESCO's International Year of Indigenous Languages", "from an Indigenous perspective, but its ultimate goal is to encourage people everywhere to embrace the spirit of inclusion and belonging." Twin Flames are Chelsey June, a Métis woman from Ottawa, and Jaaji, Inuk and Mohawk from the Arctic.
% dossier-elements: Not transcribed here; read it from the material on the CBC page. CBC offers teaching videos for both the Indigenous-language and the English versions.
% dossier-practice: Learn the Inuktitut from the artists' teaching video. The guide says past Challenge songs like this one cannot be entered in the competition but remain teaching repertoire.
% dossier-accompaniment: The guide (p. 6) asks that traditional Indigenous instruments be left to Indigenous people: "Our instruments are considered sacred" and, if an educator wishes to include them, "it is highly recommended to reserve that role for an Indigenous person, whether that be a student that is knowledgeable on the matter or bringing in a Knowledge Keeper from your community." The Band plays nothing for this record; no hand drum, rattle or powwow drum from us.
% dossier-lyrics: The words are the songwriters', in Inuktitut and English; not copied here.
% dossier-honesty: Composed and owned: a 2019 song by living songwriters, not traditional. Nothing here is ours.
% dossier-licence: Te Credit wording to use: "Human" by Twin Flames (Chelsey June and Jaaji); official anthem of UNESCO's International Year of Indigenous Languages (2019); CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge (CBC Music and MusiCounts) (to confirm with the songwriters).
% dossier-respect: A contemporary anthem shared for classes. Credit both artists and tell the song's purpose before singing it.
% dossier-review: Twin Flames for the credit and any posting; an Inuktitut speaker for pronunciation; the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre for classroom use. None has reviewed it.
